# No scripts in bash

<!-- core -->
**No script is written in bash.** A new script, and any new logic added to an existing one, is
written in the project's own language — in the `agents` repository, Go in `flow-guard`
(`<agents repo>/stats/internal/guard/<name>.go`, registered in `guard.Registry`, tested beside it, run as
`flow-guard <name>`). A bash script still standing is ported before it is extended, never grown.
A command typed into a shell is not a script; a file committed to be run is.

The only exceptions are the ones a project declares. In `agents` they are `<agents repo>/setup.sh` and the
`<agents repo>/scripts/flow-guard` bootstrap, and `flow-guard check-no-bash` fails on any other tracked `.sh`
file or file whose first line runs bash.
<!-- /core -->

## Why

A bash script grows a second copy of logic the project's own language already carries, is tested
by a harness of its own instead of the project's test runner, and fails on the quoting, word
splitting and exit-status edges that language never has. The `agents` repository ported every
script but those two to Go; this rule keeps the next one from being written in bash.

## What it does not cover

- **A one-off command line** — a shell command an agent or operator runs, a fenced command block in
  a skill, a `make` recipe. None of them is a file committed to be run.
- **A panel reproducer** — written under the gitignored `<project>/.superpowers/sdd/reproducers/` for one
  run and never committed; `flow-guard run-reproducer` runs it.
- **Files under `<agents repo>/spectre/changes/archive/`** — history, kept as the landed change carried it.
